From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:22:30 +0200
Subject: Do not assert on an open type parameter reached through a term's info

Collecting the parts of an implicit search's type for its implicit scope
asserted that every type parameter it meets is instantiated: `wildApprox`
removes uninstantiated ones from the type. It does not look inside the info of
a term reference in that type, though, and the parts collector does. So an
inline match's given search (`summonFrom`) whose type mentions an inline
`this`-proxy typed while an enclosing call's type variables were still open
crashed with "`wildApprox` failed to remove uninstantiated value" (Soundness:
`state/next` with serpentine's transparent `/`, inside a `fuse` within
`mitigate`/`protect`, in exoskeleton's rig; stock 3.10.0 crashes too, 3.9
does not). Such a parameter's declared bound stands for it, as for any other.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude Code).

Proscala-Patch: implicitparts
---
 compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/typer/Implicits.scala | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/typer/Implicits.scala b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/typer/Implicits.scala
index 32bce843e2b0cf08689f40fb2e14479e23e912ce..83db3785bb593cc936ce42ee83ed53ea703a537d 100644
--- a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/typer/Implicits.scala
+++ b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/typer/Implicits.scala
@@ -681,7 +681,11 @@ trait ImplicitRunInfo:
             case t: ConstantType =>
               traverse(t.underlying)
             case t: TypeParamRef =>
-              assert(!ctx.typerState.constraint.contains(t), i"`wildApprox` failed to remove uninstantiated $t")
+              // `wildApprox` removes uninstantiated type parameters from the type itself,
+              // but one can still be reached through the info of a term reference in it
+              // (an inline `this`-proxy typed while an enclosing call's type variables are
+              // open, for instance); its declared bound stands for it here, as it does for
+              // an instantiated one.
               traverse(t.underlying)
             case t: TermParamRef =>
               traverse(t.underlying)
